# Module 1: Hardening and Securing a Linux Server under VMware Workstation

## Introduction

This module takes a default Ubuntu server and turns it into an auditable, defensible one. We measure the result with Lynis, and every change is documented so it can be reproduced and later automated with Ansible.

Point 1 builds the foundation. A hardened server is only meaningful if the lab it lives in is controlled, so the network design comes before any configuration.

---

# POINT 1: Lab architecture, network isolation & VM provisioning

## 1. Security rationale

| Decision | Why |
|---|---|
| **Dedicated isolated network, no host adapter** | The host cannot reach the lab, and the lab cannot reach the host. A compromised or misconfigured VM has no path to your workstation. |
| **Bastion / Target split** | Mirrors production: one hardened entry point (Bastion) manages the workloads (Target). All admin traffic flows Bastion → Target. |
| **Target is isolated by default** | The Target only gets internet access during short, deliberate patching windows. |
| **No SSH server on the Bastion** | It is a client and tooling box. If nothing listens, nothing can be attacked. |
| **UEFI + Secure Boot** | Verifies the bootloader and kernel chain. A cheap hardening win that Lynis and CIS-style checks appreciate. |
| **Separate partitions on the Target** | Lets us apply `nodev,nosuid,noexec` per mount in Point 6 and prevents `/var/log` from filling `/`. This must be decided at install time. |
| **VMware guest isolation features off** | No clipboard, drag-and-drop or shared folders. These are host↔guest channels, and we want none. |
| **ISO checksum verification** | Supply-chain hygiene from the very first step. |

## 2. Target architecture

```
                 ┌────────────────────────── HOST (Windows) ──────────────────────────┐
                 │  VMware Workstation + docs only. No host adapter on VMnet2.        │
                 │                                                                    │
                 │     VMnet8 (NAT) ── internet                                       │
                 │        │                 ┊ (Target NIC: connected only             │
                 │        │                 ┊  during install & patching)             │
                 │   ┌────┴─────┐       ┌───┴──────┐                                  │
                 │   │ BASTION  │       │  TARGET  │                                  │
                 │   │ .10      │───────│  .20     │                                  │
                 │   └──────────┘       └──────────┘                                  │
                 │        VMnet2: 10.10.10.0/24, host-only, NO host adapter, NO DHCP  │
                 └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

| | Bastion | Target |
|---|---|---|
| Role | Control node (tools, keys, Git, later Ansible) | Server to harden |
| OS | Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS (standard) | Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS (**minimized**) |
| vCPU / RAM | 2 / 2 GB | 2 / 2 GB |
| Disk (thin) | 25 GB | 30 GB |
| NIC 1 | VMnet2, `10.10.10.10/24` | VMnet2, `10.10.10.20/24` |
| NIC 2 | NAT (DHCP) | NAT (DHCP), **disconnected after patching** |
| sshd | Not installed | Installed |

**Budget check:** 4 vCPU and 4 GB RAM total, which is about 17% of your RAM and half your logical cores. This leaves ample room for later modules (Docker, Kubernetes). Disks are thin-provisioned, so they grow only as used.

**Addressing rule:** the lab NIC has **no gateway and no DNS**. The only default route is on the NAT NIC, so isolated traffic can never leak toward the internet.

